    "result_text": "Done. The build is green.\n\n## What I changed\n\n**New step \u2014 `NormalizeAddress.java`** (one small class, one method): trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands `ST`\u2192`STREET`, `RD`\u2192`ROAD`, `AVE`\u2192`AVENUE` via word-boundary replacements, then stores the result back on the request. It rejects an address that is blank after normalization with a 400 (`MissingFieldsException`).\n\n**`owners.POST.yml`**: inserted `normalizeAddress` into the create pipeline right after `normalizeEmail`, before the uniqueness and build steps.\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\nI normalized once, at the creation boundary, and stored the canonical value \u2014 mirroring the existing `NormalizeTelephone`/`NormalizeEmail` steps. Because the persisted `address` is now the normalized form:\n\n- it is what's returned as `address`;\n- `EnsureUniqueHousehold` compares request vs. stored addresses that are both already canonical;\n- `Owner.getHouseholdId()` derives from the stored canonical address.\n\nSo every comparison uses the normalized form **without editing** the large `Owner` class, `EnsureUniqueHousehold`, or `ValidateOwnerFields`. The only touched units are one new zero-context class and a YAML wiring change \u2014 no existing function grew in complexity.\n\nTests: `AcceptanceTest` (3/3) and the full suite pass \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
